[[:db/cas 17592186265765 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265765 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$ and $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$ do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464817 :answer-field/key "I" "$R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464818 :answer-field/key "II" "$R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464819 :answer-field/key "III" "$R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$"] [:db/cas 17592186265767 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n- **I.** $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,1]$\n- **II.** $R=[-4,4]\\times [-1,1]$\n- **III.** $R=[-3,3]\\times [0,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265767 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4a/4a8671db243a19fb258f6144d6aba1b193e237ef93d45d877829f80b93bc7d36.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4a/4a8671db243a19fb258f6144d6aba1b193e237ef93d45d877829f80b93bc7d36.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-3,3]\\times [0,2]$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,1]$ and $R=[-4,4]\\times [-1,1]$ cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,1]$; $R=[-4,4]\\times [-1,1]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464820 :answer-field/key "I" "$R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,1]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464821 :answer-field/key "II" "$R=[-4,4]\\times [-1,1]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464822 :answer-field/key "III" "$R=[-3,3]\\times [0,2]$"] [:db/cas 17592186265769 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n- **I.** $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n- **II.** $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- **III.** $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,1]$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265769 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/78/78af77d697ea292385e2bd2570c8185c728f57cc70c3edcc25ecccd0e84780e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles II and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle I cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/78/78af77d697ea292385e2bd2570c8185c728f57cc70c3edcc25ecccd0e84780e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ and $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,1]$ do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$ cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464823 :answer-field/key "I" "$R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464824 :answer-field/key "II" "$R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464825 :answer-field/key "III" "$R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,1]$"] [:db/cas 17592186265782 :question/problem "![](images/c3/c319243db36af6829d83c8d5dfd7a724d884ff2506917249d324a315fd77ac28.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/c3/c319243db36af6829d83c8d5dfd7a724d884ff2506917249d324a315fd77ac28.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265782 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/e7/e79856c97ab7e4dd27bb1f476fd911c2e7d77aac6ab54fe50597b3aafbca720c.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III are equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/e7/e79856c97ab7e4dd27bb1f476fd911c2e7d77aac6ab54fe50597b3aafbca720c.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$. This function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$ and ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ are equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$; ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464832 :answer-field/key "I" "${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464833 :answer-field/key "II" "${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464834 :answer-field/key "III" "${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$"] [:db/cas 17592186265783 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8cb975b622bfe420d9418587293e83de60c297019f15e1b379bc118e079d35d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes 