 of ice cream flavors at an ice cream shop is not a binomial experiment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of ice creams sold at an ice cream shop before a chocolate flavor ice cream is sold” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464862 :answer-field/key "I" "The number of ice creams sold at an ice cream shop before a chocolate flavor ice cream is sold"] [:db/cas 17592186464863 :answer-field/key "II" "The price of a cone at an ice cream shop"] [:db/cas 17592186464864 :answer-field/key "III" "The number of ice cream flavors at an ice cream shop"] [:db/cas 17592186265917 :question/problem "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n- **I.** The number of files reviewed by an antivirus until the first infected file is found\n- **II.** The number of infected files found in a computer\n- **III.** The number of crayons randomly picked out from a case before a blue crayon is picked\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265917 :question/worked-solution "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Reviewing a file is a binomial experiment, and the number of files reviewed before the first infected file is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Finding an infected file is a binomial experiment, but the number of infected files in a computer cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Randomly picking out a crayon is a binomial experiment, and the number of crayons picked out before a blue crayon is picked can be inter