sition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$; $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464907 :answer-field/key "I" "$y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$"] [:db/cas 17592186464908 :answer-field/key "II" "$y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$"] [:db/cas 17592186464909 :answer-field/key "III" "$y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$"] [:db/cas 17592186266039 :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-7x{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$\n- **II.** $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$\n- **III.** $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-7x{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266039 :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of a known solution and a non-constant function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of a known solution and a non-constant function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464910 :answer-field/key "I" "$y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464911 :answer-field/key "II" "$y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464912 :answer-field/key "III" "$y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186266041 :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$\n- **II.** $y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$\n- **III.** $y(x)=({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464913 :answer-field/key "I" "$y(x)={e}^{x}+4{e}^{-x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464914 :answer-field/key "II" "$y(x)=5{e}^{-x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464915 :answer-field/key "III" "$y(x)=({e}^{-x}+1){e}^{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186266042 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n\nIII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266042 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464916 :answer-field/key "I" "$[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464917 :answer-field/key "II" "$[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464918 :answer-field/key "III" "$[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186266043 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[0,2]$\n- **II.** $[2,4]$\n- **III.** $[0,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose that $f(x)$