nterval $[2,4].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[0,1)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[2,4]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464919 :answer-field/key "I" "$[0,2]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464920 :answer-field/key "II" "$[2,4]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464921 :answer-field/key "III" "$[0,1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186266044 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{8}^{x}-61, & x<2 \\\\ {3}^{x-1}, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[3,5]$\n- **II.** $[1,3]$\n- **III.** $(-1,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{8}^{x}-61, & x<2 \\\\ {3}^{x-1}, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266044 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[3,5].$ In this interval, $f(x)={3}^{x-1},$ so the function is continuous on the interval $[3,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[1,3].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}({8}^{x}-61)={8}^{2}-61=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}{3}^{x-1}={3}^{2-1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=2$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,3],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[3,5].$ In this interval, $f(x)={3}^{x-1},$ so the function is continuous on the interval $[3,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[1,3].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}({8}^{x}-61)={8}^{2}-61=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}{3}^{x-1}={3}^{2-1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=2$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,3],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[3,5]$; $[1,3]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464922 :answer-field/key "I" "$[3,5]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464923 :answer-field/key "II" "$[1,3]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464924 :answer-field/key "III" "$(-1,1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186266059 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & -1\\le x<1 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $(-1,1)$\n- **II.** $[-2,0]$\n- **III.** $[0,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & -1\\le x<1 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186266059 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[-2,0].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}(-1)=-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{3})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=-1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-2,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{3})=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[-2,0].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}(-1)=-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{3})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=-1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-2,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{3})=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-2,0]$; $[0,2]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464925 :answer-field/key "I" "$(-1,1)$"] [:db/cas 17592186464926 :answer-field/key "II" "$[-2,0]$"] [:db/cas 17592186464927 :answer-field/key "III" "$[0,2]$"] [:db/cas 17592186266062 :question/problem "![](images/e3/e30919dc7cc107bdda9872079b4dc0e59fe8e3e5767aa2d407b9d0af392803e0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- **III.** $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- **IV.** $F$ is solenoidal" "![](images/e3/e30919dc7cc107bdda9872079b4dc0e59fe8e3e5767aa2d407b9d0af392803e0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266062 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{div}\\,F>0$ is true, whereas statements $\\text{div}\\,F=0$ and $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{div}\\,F>0$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186438941 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\text{div}\\,F=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438942 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\text{div}\\,F>0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438943 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\text{div}\\,F<0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438944 :answer-field/key "IV" "$F$ is solenoidal"] [:db/cas 17592186266063 :question/problem "![](images/24/243496443c3878c90fb67888add6c6ad6d3e69aa20ba24f42ee89d58a2742534.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- **III.** $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- **IV.** $F$ is solenoidal" "![](images/24/243496443c3878c90fb67888add6c6ad6d3e69aa20ba24f42ee89d58a2742534.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266063 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{div}\\,F=0$ is true, whereas statements $\\text{div}\\,F>0$ and $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{div}\\,F=0$; “$F$ is solenoidal” only."] [:db/cas 17592186438945 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\text{div}\\,F=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438946 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\text{div}\\,F>0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438947 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\text{div}\\,F<0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438948 :answer-field/key "IV" "$F$ is solenoidal"] [:db/cas 17592186266079 :question/problem "![](images/17/1709a94b7361a1e8764d94199f9e84802ae925a09e6481250d0846b83bb6549f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- **III.** $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- **IV.** $F$ is irrotational" "![](images/17/1709a94b7361a1e8764d94199f9e84802ae925a09e6481250d0846b83bb6549f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266079 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/7c/7c831425eae7afd709d546a8e05100f495adc04bba6450cfa56d370b415a28f5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/7c/7c831425eae7afd709d546a8e05100f495adc04bba6450cfa56d370b415a28f5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$ is true, whereas statement $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction” is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement “$F$ is irrotational” is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$; “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction” only."] [:db/cas 17592186438957 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\text{curl}\\,F=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438958 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438959 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction."] [:db/cas 17592186438960 :answer-field/key "IV" "$F$ is irrotational"] [:db/cas 17592186266080 :question/problem "![](images/78/781a4e33b8b55762b2dcb7575d08315b5842f83d6d78a5136a1c353eefd3069d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- **III.** $F$ is irrotational\n- **IV.** $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." "![](images/78/781a4e33b8b55762b2dcb7575d08315b5842f83d6d78a5136a1c353eefd3069d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266080 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/97/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/97/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ is true, whereas statement $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$ is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement “$F$ is irrotational” is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction” is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$; “$F$ is irrotational” only."] [:db/cas 17592186438961 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\text{curl}\\,F=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438962 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$"] [:db/cas 17592186438963 :answer-field/key "III" "$F$ is irrotational"] [:db/cas 17592186438964 :answer-field/key "IV" "$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction."] [:db/cas 17592186266090 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186464928 :answer-field/key "A" "$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464929 :answer-field/key "B" "$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464930 :answer-field/key "C" "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186266113 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=-1 \\\\ 2x+8y &=-2 \\\\ -3x-12y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is inconsistent\n\n- **II.** The system is consistent dependent\n\n- **III.** The system has a unique solution" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=-1 \\\\ 2x+8y &=-2 \\\\ -3x-12y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266113 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement “The system is consistent dependent” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186438968 :answer-field/key "I" "The system is inconsistent"] [:db/cas 17592186438969 :answer-field/key "II" "The system is consistent dependent"] [:db/cas 17592186438970 :answer-field/key "III" "The system has a unique solution"] [:db/cas 17592186266114 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=3 \\\\ -3x+3y-3z &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is inconsistent\n- **II.** The system has a unique solution\n- **III.** The system is consistent dependent" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=3 \\\\ -3x+3y-3z &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266114 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y+0z=19,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y+0z=19,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement “The system is inconsistent” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186438980 :answer-field/key "I" "The system is inconsistent"] [:db/cas 17592186438981 :answer-field/key "II" "The system has a unique solution"] [:db/cas 17592186438982 :answer-field/key "III" "The system is consistent dependent"] [:db/cas 17592186266115 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=2 \\\\ -3x-4y &=3 \\\\ 2x-4y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system has a unique solution\n- **III.** The system is inconsistent" "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=2 \\\\ -3x-4y &=3 \\\\ 2x-4y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186266115 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=-5,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=-5,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement “The system is inconsistent” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186438983 :answer-field/key "I" "The system is consistent dependent"] [:db/cas 17592186438984 :answer-field/key "II" "The system has a unique solution"] [:db/cas 17592186438985 :answer-field/key "III" "The system is inconsistent"] [:db/cas 17592186268906 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- **III.** ${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186268906 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,0)=0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-1)\\cdot (-1))=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-1)}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,-1,1)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,-1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot (-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement “The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,0)=0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-1)\\cdot (-1))=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-1)}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined” is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined” is also false. If we substitute $(1,-1,1)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,-1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot (-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450702 :answer-field/key "I" "The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$"] [:db/cas 17592186450703 :answer-field/key "II" "${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined"] [:db/cas 17592186450704 :answer-field/key "III" "${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined"] [:db/cas 17592186268907 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186268907 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(4,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(4,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(4,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,2) & =\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}_{y}(4,2) & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,2)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,2)=\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{2}}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,2)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(4,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(4,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(4,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,2) & =\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}_{y}(4,2) & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined” is false. If we substitute $(0,2)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,2)=\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{2}}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,2)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$”; “${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450705 :answer-field/key "I" "Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186450706 :answer-field/key "II" "${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined"] [:db/cas 17592186450707 :answer-field/key "III" "${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined"] [:db/cas 17592186268914 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186268914 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined” is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined” is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450708 :answer-field/key "I" "Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$"] [:db/cas 17592186450709 :answer-field/key "II" "${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined"] [:db/cas 17592186450710 :answer-field/key "III" "${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined"] [:db/cas 17592186268926 :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6x{y}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186268926 :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2})=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy)=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y)=8y-15{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-5{x}^{3})=8y-15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=18{x}^{2}y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{3})=6{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2})=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy)=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y)=8y-15{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-5{x}^{3})=8y-15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=18{x}^{2}y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{3})=6{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$”; “${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464931 :answer-field/key "I" "${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$"] [:db/cas 17592186464932 :answer-field/key "II" "${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464933 :answer-field/key "III" "${f}_{x}(x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6x{y}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186268927 :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x}$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{e}^{y+1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y+1}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=7x-3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-7y$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186268927 :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7x-3y)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-7y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7x-3y)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-7y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x}$”; “${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{e}^{y+1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y+1}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464934 :answer-field/key "I" "${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464935 :answer-field/key "II" "${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{e}^{y+1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y+1}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464936 :answer-field/key "III" "${f}_{x}(x,y)=7x-3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-7y$"] [:db/cas 17592186268966 :question/problem "![](images/57/5768d8f90d3dd754471a69d25582eca6cdb2f4993b9b22020dc9f2038aa61954.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above summarizes data collected by a Midwestern high school showing the relationship between the number of absences in a month and the class size. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Only one class reported a total of $6$ absences in the month.\n- Two classes have a total of $20$ students each.\n- Only one class has more than $24$ students.\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/57/5768d8f90d3dd754471a69d25582eca6cdb2f4993b9b22020dc9f2038aa61954.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above summarizes data collected by a Midwestern high school showing the relationship between the number of absences in a month and the class size. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186268966 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is false. The scatter plot has two points whose vertical coordinate is equal to $6,$ namely, $(16,6)$ and $(20,6).$ This means that two of the classes each reported a total of 6 absences in the month.\n\n![](images/a6/a62b1d5a63764b38013fdbdba9ba85a4495cd996a2d9f975dbe3d7eb89166234.png)\n\n  \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $20,$ namely, $(20,6).$ This means that only one of the classes has a total of  $20$ students.\n\n![](images/3c/3c41f2e111ef141511ec4f2faa655ddcea8f2b7e0b73bd37f0b1e142d5d60f14.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. From the scatter plot, only one class has more than $24$ students. This class is represented by the point to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/ad/adcba37370fdac69b152a8d18ef710d5f665d9a3252376ea312808be7d08a407.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement “Only one class reported a total of $6$ absences in the month” is false. The scatter plot has two points whose vertical coordinate is equal to $6,$ namely, $(16,6)$ and $(20,6).$ This means that two of the classes each reported a total of 6 absences in the month.\n\n![](images/a6/a62b1d5a63764b38013fdbdba9ba85a4495cd996a2d9f975dbe3d7eb89166234.png)\n\n  \n\n- Statement “Two classes have a total of $20$ students each” is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $20,$ namely, $(20,6).$ This means that only one of the classes has a total of  $20$ students.\n\n![](images/3c/3c41f2e111ef141511ec4f2faa655ddcea8f2b7e0b73bd37f0b1e142d5d60f14.png)\n\n- Statement “Only one class has more than $24$ students” is true. From the scatter plot, only one class has more than $24$ students. This class is represented by the point to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/ad/adcba37370fdac69b152a8d18ef710d5f665d9a3252376ea312808be7d08a407.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Only one class has more than $24$ students” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464937 :answer-field/key "I" "Only one class reported a total of $6$ absences in the month."] [:db/cas 17592186464938 :answer-field/key "II" "Two classes have a total of $20$ students each."] [:db/cas 17592186464939 :answer-field/key "III" "Only one class has more than $24$ students."] [:db/cas 17592186268967 :question/problem "![](images/69/69517e814dae28db96c64a662ce97b5fc40ea10355cb5a02561a0d12e1a3f845.png)\n\nA math teacher asked his students how many hours of sleep they had the\nnight before a test. The information he received is summarized in the scatter plot above.\n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Only one student obtained a score of $90.$\n- Only one student slept for $6$ hours.\n- None of the students obtained a score lower than $75.$\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/69/69517e814dae28db96c64a662ce97b5fc40ea10355cb5a02561a0d12e1a3f845.png)\n\nA math teacher asked his students how many hours of sleep they had the\nnight before a test. The information he received is summarized in the scatter plot above.\n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186268967 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $90,$ namely, $(7,90).$ This means that only one student obtained a score of $90.$\n\n![](images/ef/ef915015fa9c7685f45f26ee9cd2c10dfce5a4253aed22b6ea2ea57f0c3c26e2.png)\n\n    \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $6,$ namely, $(6,80)$ and $(6,85).$ This means that two students had  $6$ hours of sleep.\n\n![](images/31/3139ca71a780b76ca7355ddd119d6502f1cd390d67064db3a4a57801e33f2917.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, three students obtained a score lower than  $75.$  Those students are represented by the three dots below the horizontal line.\n\n![](images/a6/a675a94a9183a86d221bb2199dae38f9f55b9f91dff9868320b0b8f4d38b3fe9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement “Only one student obtained a score of $90.$” is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $90,$ namely, $(7,90).$ This means that only one student obtained a score of $90.$\n\n![](images/ef/ef915015fa9c7685f45f26ee9cd2c10dfce5a4253aed22b6ea2ea57f0c3c26e2.png)\n\n    \n\n- Statement “Only one student slept for $6$ hours” is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $6,$ namely, $(6,80)$ and $(6,85).$ This means that two students had  $6$ hours of sleep.\n\n![](images/31/3139ca71a780b76ca7355ddd119d6502f1cd390d67064db3a4a57801e33f2917.png)\n\n- Statement “None of the students obtained a score lower than $75.$” is false. From the scatter plot, three students obtained a score lower than  $75.$  Those students are represented by the three dots below the horizontal line.\n\n![](images/a6/a675a94a9183a86d221bb2199dae38f9f55b9f91dff9868320b0b8f4d38b3fe9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Only one student obtained a score of $90.$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464940 :answer-field/key "I" "Only one student obtained a score of $90.$"] [:db/cas 17592186464941 :answer-field/key "II" "Only one student slept for $6$ hours."] [:db/cas 17592186464942 :answer-field/key "III" "None of the students obtained a score lower than $75.$"] [:db/cas 17592186268968 :question/problem "![](images/37/376bd65db6b1d0a91c6013462e686ffad4d8acceee4948e61cf291d6403a12cd.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above is based on data collected from several homes in a given community. It shows the relationship between the age of a house and the number of people living in it. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There is only one house where precisely $3$ people live.\n- Two of the houses are $16$ years old.\n- Only one house is older than $24$ years old.\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/37/376bd65db6b1d0a91c6013462e686ffad4d8acceee4948e61cf291d6403a12cd.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above is based on data collected from several homes in a given community. It shows the relationship between the age of a house and the number of people living in it. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186268968 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $3,$ namely, $(18,3).$ This means that there is only one house where $3$ people live.\n\n![](images/7a/7a56236f0c5649a64436809b2a5e1fb8f00ed3d345cd81c571f96d579d6c19b6.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $16,$ namely, $(16,6).$ This means that only one house is $16$ years old.\n\n![](images/6d/6d9a60cd10c6d7cc9f9d51d0be44c32cb688e19da988b3e84b30f0c4be32a2a8.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, two houses are more than $24$ years old, represented by the two dots to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/44/44b7395bb2e5ee0a71899869e6caedb949e8744adde314134f6a09e4c8f8a35d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement “There is only one house where precisely $3$ people live” is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $3,$ namely, $(18,3).$ This means that there is only one house where $3$ people live.\n\n![](images/7a/7a56236f0c5649a64436809b2a5e1fb8f00ed3d345cd81c571f96d579d6c19b6.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement “Two of the houses are $16$ years old” is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $16,$ namely, $(16,6).$ This means that only one house is $16$ years old.\n\n![](images/6d/6d9a60cd10c6d7cc9f9d51d0be44c32cb688e19da988b3e84b30f0c4be32a2a8.png)\n\n- Statement “Only one house is older than $24$ years old” is false. From the scatter plot, two houses are more than $24$ years old, represented by the two dots to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/44/44b7395bb2e5ee0a71899869e6caedb949e8744adde314134f6a09e4c8f8a35d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “There is only one house where precisely $3$ people live” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464943 :answer-field/key "I" "There is only one house where precisely $3$ people live."] [:db/cas 17592186464944 :answer-field/key "II" "Two of the houses are $16$ years old."] [:db/cas 17592186464945 :answer-field/key "III" "Only one house is older than $24$ years old."] [:db/cas 17592186269024 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186269024 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2.4}{7}+\\frac{0.6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{7}+\\frac{3.4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a stochastic vector” is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement $Pq=q$ is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.85\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2.4}{7}+\\frac{0.6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{7}+\\frac{3.4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$” is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$q$ is a stochastic vector”; $Pq=q$; “$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$”."] [:db/cas 17592186464946 :answer-field/key "I" "$q$ is a stochastic vector"] [:db/cas 17592186464947 :answer-field/key "II" "$Pq=q$"] [:db/cas 17592186464948 :answer-field/key "III" "$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$"] [:db/cas 17592186269025 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186269025 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.02+0.24 \\\\ 0.18+0.56\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.26 \\\\ 0.74\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a stochastic vector” is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement $Pq=q$ is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.02+0.24 \\\\ 0.18+0.56\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.26 \\\\ 0.74\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$” is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$q$ is a stochastic vector” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464949 :answer-field/key "I" "$q$ is a stochastic vector"] [:db/cas 17592186464950 :answer-field/key "II" "$Pq=q$"] [:db/cas 17592186464951 :answer-field/key "III" "$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$"] [:db/cas 17592186269026 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186269026 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1.2}{9}+\\frac{0.5}{9} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{9}+\\frac{1.2}{9}+\\frac{0.2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2.4}{9}+\\frac{1.6}{9}+\\frac{0.3}{9}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2.7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4.3}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a stochastic vector” is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement $Pq=q$ is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1.2}{9}+\\frac{0.5}{9} \\\\ \\frac{0.6}{9}+\\frac{1.2}{9}+\\frac{0.2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2.4}{9}+\\frac{1.6}{9}+\\frac{0.3}{9}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2.7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{4.3}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$” is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$q$ is a stochastic vector” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464952 :answer-field/key "I" "$q$ is a stochastic vector"] [:db/cas 17592186464953 :answer-field/key "II" "$Pq=q$"] [:db/cas 17592186464954 :answer-field/key "III" "$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$"] [:db/cas 17592186269027 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186269027 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.03+0.105+0.12 \\\\ 0.06+0.49+0.07 \\\\ 0.01+0.105+0.01\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.255 \\\\ 0.62 \\\\ 0.125\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a stochastic vector” is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement $Pq=q$ is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.15 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.7 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.15 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.7 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.03+0.105+0.12 \\\\ 0.06+0.49+0.07 \\\\ 0.01+0.105+0.01\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.255 \\\\ 0.62 \\\\ 0.125\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$” is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$q$ is a stochastic vector” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464955 :answer-field/key "I" "$q$ is a stochastic vector"] [:db/cas 17592186464956 :answer-field/key "II" "$Pq=q$"] [:db/cas 17592186464957 :answer-field/key "III" "$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$"] [:db/cas 17592186269073 :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464958 :answer-field/key "U" "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464959 :answer-field/key "V" "$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464960 :answer-field/key "W" "$W=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186269218 :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite\n- **II.** $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $T(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite" "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186269218 :question/worked-solution "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{1}) & =1\\cdot 1=1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{2}) & =1\\cdot (-1)=-1<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement “$R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement “$S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x$ is negative-definite” is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$T(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite” is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{1}) & =1\\cdot 1=1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{2}) & =1\\cdot (-1)=-1<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x$ is negative-definite” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450711 :answer-field/key "I" "$R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite"] [:db/cas 17592186450712 :answer-field/key "II" "$S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x$ is negative-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186450713 :answer-field/key "III" "$T(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186269221 :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186269221 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form “${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$” is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form “${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$” is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form “${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$” is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464961 :answer-field/key "I" "${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186464962 :answer-field/key "II" "${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186464963 :answer-field/key "III" "${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$"] [:db/cas 17592186269222 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is indefinite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative semi-definite" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186269222 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, by computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since both eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive semi-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$” is true. Indeed, by computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is indefinite” and “$A$ is negative semi-definite” are false. Since both eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive semi-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450714 :answer-field/key "I" "The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$"] [:db/cas 17592186450715 :answer-field/key "II" "${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is indefinite"] [:db/cas 17592186450716 :answer-field/key "III" "$A$ is negative semi-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186269223 :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186269223 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form “${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$” is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form “${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$” is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form “${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$” is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$”; “${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464964 :answer-field/key "I" "${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186464965 :answer-field/key "II" "${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$"] [:db/cas 17592186464966 :answer-field/key "III" "${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$"] [:db/cas 17592186269224 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive semi-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is indefinite" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186269224 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$” is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive semi-definite” is false, while statement “$A$ is indefinite” is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is indefinite” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450717 :answer-field/key "I" "The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$"] [:db/cas 17592186450718 :answer-field/key "II" "${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive semi-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186450719 :answer-field/key "III" "$A$ is indefinite"] [:db/cas 17592186269225 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186269225 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and  III are false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, neither the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ nor the matrix $A$ is positive-definite or negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statements “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” and  “$A$ is negative-definite” are false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, neither the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ nor the matrix $A$ is positive-definite or negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450720 :answer-field/key "I" "${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$"] [:db/cas 17592186450721 :answer-field/key "II" "${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186450722 :answer-field/key "III" "$A$ is negative-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186271977 :question/problem "Suppose $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n- **I.** $a\\cdot a<0$\n- **II.** $b\\cdot a>0$\n- **III.** $b\\cdot a=5$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Suppose $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186271977 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II and III are false. Simplifying the expression $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (a+b) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $b\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"." "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $a\\cdot a<0$ is false. Indeed, $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement $b\\cdot a>0$ and $b\\cdot a=5$ are false. Simplifying the expression $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (a+b) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $b\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."] [:db/cas 17592186464967 :answer-field/key "I" "$a\\cdot a<0$"] [:db/cas 17592186464968 :answer-field/key "II" "$b\\cdot a>0$"] [:db/cas 17592186464969 :answer-field/key "III" "$b\\cdot a=5$"] [:db/cas 17592186271986 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.$\n\n- **II.** If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.$\n\n- **III.** If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.$" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186271986 :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The matrix $B$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.$” is false. The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.$” is true. The matrix $B$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.$” is true. The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.$”; “If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450723 :answer-field/key "I" "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186450724 :answer-field/key "II" "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186450725 :answer-field/key "III" "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186271990 :question/problem "Let $⟨x,3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}kx+3y &=1 \\\\ 7x-2y &=8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. $-3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nII. $3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nIII. $|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=3|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $⟨x,3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}kx+3y &=1 \\\\ 7x-2y &=8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186271990 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464970 :answer-field/key "I" "$-3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$"] [:db/cas 17592186464971 :answer-field/key "II" "$3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$"] [:db/cas 17592186464972 :answer-field/key "III" "$|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=3|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$"] [:db/cas 17592186271991 :question/problem "Let $⟨x,-2⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}15x+8y &=-1 \\\\ kx+13y &=-31.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n- **I.** $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=2|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- **II.** $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=-4|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- **III.** $-2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $⟨x,-2⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}15x+8y &=-1 \\\\ kx+13y &=-31.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186271991 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ -2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ -2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $-2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464973 :answer-field/key "I" "$|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=2|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$"] [:db/cas 17592186464974 :answer-field/key "II" "$|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=-4|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$"] [:db/cas 17592186464975 :answer-field/key "III" "$-2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$"] [:db/cas 17592186272012 :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n- **I.** $A$ is a triangular matrix\n- **II.** $A$ does not have identical rows\n- **III.** $A$ does not have proportional columns\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186272012 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ is not a triangular matrix.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\nHence, if $|A|\\ne 0$ then $A$ doesn't have proportional nor identical rows/columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is a triangular matrix” is false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ is not a triangular matrix.\n\n- Statements “$A$ does not have identical rows” and “$A$ does not have proportional columns” are true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\nHence, if $|A|\\ne 0$ then $A$ doesn't have proportional nor identical rows/columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ does not have identical rows”; “$A$ does not have proportional columns” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464976 :answer-field/key "I" "$A$ is a triangular matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186464977 :answer-field/key "II" "$A$ does not have identical rows"] [:db/cas 17592186464978 :answer-field/key "III" "$A$ does not have proportional columns"] [:db/cas 17592186272013 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n- **I.** $A$ has proportional columns\n- **II.** $A$ has identical columns\n- **III.** $A$ has identical rows\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186272013 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has proportional columns” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464979 :answer-field/key "I" "$A$ has proportional columns"] [:db/cas 17592186464980 :answer-field/key "II" "$A$ has identical columns"] [:db/cas 17592186464981 :answer-field/key "III" "$A$ has identical rows"] [:db/cas 17592186272020 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- **II.** $T$ is invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(T)=7$" "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272020 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Indeed, since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=7$ is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186450726 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$"] [:db/cas 17592186450727 :answer-field/key "II" "$T$ is invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186450728 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\text{rank}(T)=7$"] [:db/cas 17592186272021 :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n- **II.** $T$ is invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272021 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$ is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ is true. Indeed, since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$T$ is invertible”; $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186464982 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464983 :answer-field/key "II" "$T$ is invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186464984 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\text{nullity}(T)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186272063 :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- **II.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272063 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186450729 :answer-field/key "I" "$[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$"] [:db/cas 17592186450730 :answer-field/key "II" "$[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$"] [:db/cas 17592186450731 :answer-field/key "III" "$[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$"] [:db/cas 17592186272064 :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- **II.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272064 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186450732 :answer-field/key "I" "$[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$"] [:db/cas 17592186450733 :answer-field/key "II" "$[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$"] [:db/cas 17592186450734 :answer-field/key "III" "$[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$"] [:db/cas 17592186272087 :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}$ and $g(t)=3{t}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{4}$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}$ and $g(t)=3{t}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186272087 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u={t}^{3}+t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u={t}^{3}+t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$ is true, while statement $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{4}$ is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement “$f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$” is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$; “$f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186464985 :answer-field/key "I" "$⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186464986 :answer-field/key "II" "$⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{4}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464987 :answer-field/key "III" "$f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$"] [:db/cas 17592186272092 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- **III.** The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272092 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-\\lambda )\\cdot (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -2)\\lambda (\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =5,$ $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-4$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-\\lambda )\\cdot (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -2)\\lambda (\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =5,$ $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-4$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$”; “$\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450735 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$"] [:db/cas 17592186450736 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$"] [:db/cas 17592186450737 :answer-field/key "III" "The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$"] [:db/cas 17592186272104 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n- **U.** $U={\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **V.** $V={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **W.** $W={\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464988 :answer-field/key "U" "$U={\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464989 :answer-field/key "V" "$V={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464990 :answer-field/key "W" "$W={\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186272106 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n- **U.** $U={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **V.** $V={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **W.** $W={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464991 :answer-field/key "U" "$U={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464992 :answer-field/key "V" "$V={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464993 :answer-field/key "W" "$W={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186272107 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n- **U.** $U={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **V.** $V={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **W.** $W={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186464994 :answer-field/key "U" "$U={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464995 :answer-field/key "V" "$V={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186464996 :answer-field/key "W" "$W={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [:db/cas 17592186272112 :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186272112 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-(e-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=-{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-({e}^{-1}-e) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-(e-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=-{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-({e}^{-1}-e) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186464997 :answer-field/key "I" "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product"] [:db/cas 17592186464998 :answer-field/key "II" "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product"] [:db/cas 17592186464999 :answer-field/key "III" "$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product"] [:db/cas 17592186272117 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${A}^{T}=A$\n- **II.** ${A}^{2}=A$\n- **III.** $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272117 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since ${A}^{T}\\ne A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since not both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement ${A}^{T}=A$ is false since ${A}^{T}\\ne A.$\n\n- Statement ${A}^{2}=A$ is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix” is false since not both statements ${A}^{T}=A$ and ${A}^{2}=A$ are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" ${A}^{2}=A$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186450738 :answer-field/key "I" "${A}^{T}=A$"] [:db/cas 17592186450739 :answer-field/key "II" "${A}^{2}=A$"] [:db/cas 17592186450740 :answer-field/key "III" "$A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186272132 :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2(t-1),\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- **II.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- **III.** $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2(t-1),\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272132 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1)({e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2(t-1) & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=2, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2(t-1){e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-(-2))-2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+2-(2{e}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{(t-1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =e-e \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1)({e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2(t-1) & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=2, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2(t-1){e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-(-2))-2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+2-(2{e}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{(t-1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =e-e \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450741 :answer-field/key "I" "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product"] [:db/cas 17592186450742 :answer-field/key "II" "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product"] [:db/cas 17592186450743 :answer-field/key "III" "$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product"] [:db/cas 17592186272134 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrix $Q$ and the $n\\times n$ matrix $P?$\n- **I.** If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n- **II.** If the determinant of $P$ is either $1$ or $-1,$ then $P$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** $Q-I$ is singular\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrix $Q$ and the $n\\times n$ matrix $P?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186272134 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $\\lambda $ is an eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every orthogonal matrix has the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$, but not every matrix with the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$ is orthogonal. As a counterexample, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P)=2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n however, $P$ is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following orthogonal matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nQ-I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand $Q-I$ is not singular since, for example, it doesn't have proportional rows or columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$” is true. If $\\lambda $ is an eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n\n- Statement “If the determinant of $P$ is either $1$ or $-1,$ then $P$ is orthogonal” is false. Every orthogonal matrix has the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$, but not every matrix with the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$ is orthogonal. As a counterexample, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P)=2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n however, $P$ is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement “$Q-I$ is singular” is false. As a counterexample, consider the following orthogonal matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nQ-I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand $Q-I$ is not singular since, for example, it doesn't have proportional rows or columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186465000 :answer-field/key "I" "If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$"] [:db/cas 17592186465001 :answer-field/key "II" "If the determinant of $P$ is either $1$ or $-1,$ then $P$ is orthogonal"] [:db/cas 17592186465002 :answer-field/key "III" "$Q-I$ is singular"] [:db/cas 17592186272158 :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n- **I.** $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- **II.** $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- **III.** $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$\n\nSelect all that apply." "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272158 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$ **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$ could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$ **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186465003 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186465004 :answer-field/key "II" "$2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186465005 :answer-field/key "III" "$5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$"] [:db/cas 17592186272159 :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272159 :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “$\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation” is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$” is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation”; “$A$ is invertible” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450744 :answer-field/key "I" "$\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation"] [:db/cas 17592186450745 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186450746 :answer-field/key "III" "$A$ is invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186272160 :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272160 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement “$(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution” is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$ and $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$ are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450747 :answer-field/key "I" "$(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution"] [:db/cas 17592186450748 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186450749 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186272161 :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-7I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A-7I$ is singular\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-7I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272161 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-7I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-7I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-7I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-7I$ is singular, then $(A-7I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements “$A-7I$ is singular” is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-7I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=0$ is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-7I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-7I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$” is true. Notice that if $M=A-7I$ is singular, then $(A-7I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A-7I$ is singular”; “$\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450750 :answer-field/key "I" "$A-7I$ is singular"] [:db/cas 17592186450751 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186450752 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186272162 :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A+8I$ is singular\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$" "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186272162 :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+8I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that if $M=A+8I$ is singular, then $(A+8I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+8I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+8I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements “$A+8I$ is singular” is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+8I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement “$\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$” is true. Notice that if $M=A+8I$ is singular, then $(A+8I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$ is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+8I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+8I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A+8I$ is singular”; “$\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450753 :answer-field/key "I" "$A+8I$ is singular"] [:db/cas 17592186450754 :answer-field/key "II" "$\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$"] [:db/cas 17592186450755 :answer-field/key "III" "$\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$"] [:db/cas 17592186272168 :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,S=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 