 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $f(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$”; “$f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$”; “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$”."] [:db/cas 17592186450756 :answer-field/key "I" "$f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186450757 :answer-field/key "II" "$f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186450758 :answer-field/key "III" "$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186272183 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-27,\\, & x<3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2),\\, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=3$\