$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=3$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}h(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =h(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$h(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$” is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=3,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=3$”; “$h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$”; “$h(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$”."] [:db/cas 17592186450759 :answer-field/key "I" "$h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=3$"] [:db/cas 17592186450760 :answer-field/key "II" "$h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$"] [:db/cas 17592186450761 :answer-field/key "III" "$h(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$"] [:db/cas 17592186272185 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x<0 \\\\ -1, & x=0 \\\\ x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **I.** $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- **II.** $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- **III.** $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x<0 \\\\ -1, & x=0 \\\\ x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186272185 :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & =\\frac{1}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=0$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & =\\frac{1}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=0$” is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=0$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186465009 :answer-field/key "I" "$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186465010 :answer-field/key "II" "$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186465011 :answer-field/key "III" "$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186272186 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are tru