ed}-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2} & =-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}=0.$ is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2} & =-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent” is true. Since statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$”; “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent” only."] [:db/cas 17592186450841 :answer-field/key "I" "$-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186450842 :answer-field/key "II" "${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186450843 :answer-field/key "III" "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent."] [:db/cas 17592186275388 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275388 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent” is false. Since statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186450844 :answer-field/key "I" "$4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186450845 :answer-field/key "II" "${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186450846 :answer-field/key "III" "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent."] [:db/cas 17592186275389 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2y=3x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2y=3x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275389 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $2y=3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =3x \\\\ 2(0) & =3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a+\\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(\\frac{3}{2}a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $2y=3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =3x \\\\ 2(0) & =3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a+\\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(\\frac{3}{2}a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ contains the zero-vector”; “$L$ is closed under vector addition”; “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only."] [:db/cas 17592186465165 :answer-field/key "I" "$L$ contains the zero-vector"] [:db/cas 17592186465166 :answer-field/key "II" "$L$ is closed under vector addition"] [:db/cas 17592186465167 :answer-field/key "III" "$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication"] [:db/cas 17592186465168 :answer-field/key "IV" "$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186275390 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275390 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ contains the zero-vector”; “$L$ is closed under vector addition”; “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only."] [:db/cas 17592186465169 :answer-field/key "I" "$L$ contains the zero-vector"] [:db/cas 17592186465170 :answer-field/key "II" "$L$ is closed under vector addition"] [:db/cas 17592186465171 :answer-field/key "III" "$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication"] [:db/cas 17592186465172 :answer-field/key "IV" "$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186275391 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-2,3).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-2,3).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275391 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b) \\\\ 3a+3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -2(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(-2a) \\\\ k(3a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ -2(ka) \\\\ 3(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b) \\\\ 3a+3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -2(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(-2a) \\\\ k(3a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ -2(ka) \\\\ 3(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186465173 :answer-field/key "I" "$L$ contains the zero-vector"] [:db/cas 17592186465174 :answer-field/key "II" "$L$ is closed under vector addition"] [:db/cas 17592186465175 :answer-field/key "III" "$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication"] [:db/cas 17592186465176 :answer-field/key "IV" "$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186275392 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,3,-2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,3,-2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275392 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4a+4b \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ -2(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(4a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ k(-2a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ -2(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2